  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather with gradually increasing \ntemperatures through the end of the week under high pressure.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure continues to settle over the area through the late\nweek, returning a subsidence pattern to the CWA which will help\nclear skies and lead to a drier weather pattern. As the center\nof the ridge axis slowly moves into the area, an increased\npressure gradient aloft will allow for winds to be gusty through\nthe day today. The ridge axis will settle over the state by\ntonight, and calmer winds are in store for the rest of the week.\n\nSince the high pressure is not fully established over the entire\nforecast area this afternoon, there is a slight chance for some\nafternoon diurnally-driven showers across the St. John Valley.\nAny rainfall from these showers will be light as there will not\nbe very much instability to support these showers, and PWATs\nwill be rapidly dropping as the drier air mass moves into the\narea, limiting moisture availability.\n\nTemperatures will gradually increase through the end of the\nweek. Today will be the coolest day, with highs only in the low\nto mid 70s. By this weekend, daily high temperatures will lift\njust above average, reaching into the low to mid 80s. Overnight,\nclear skies and calm winds tonight will allow for effective \nradiational cooling, and lows could dip to around 50 Downeast,\nand into the upper 40s across the north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Potential for a system moving in early next \nweek to bring another round of wetting rainfall.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nOn Monday next week, a shortwave trough approaches from the \nwest, bringing the next round of moisture into the area that \ncould bring a chance of showers and thunderstorms. A more \norganized system which could produce a wetting rainfall is \npossible Tuesday into Wednesday though details are still \nuncertain as to how much precipitation is expected.",
